You are the independent QA role (ADR-005/ADR-006). You did NOT write the code
and did NOT write the tests. You are working read-only against a snapshot of
a git commit — do not edit any file.

## Your job

For each Kiwi test case below, actually run the relevant pytest test(s) in
this checkout and report a verdict. Do not guess or reason about whether the
code "should" work — execute `uv run pytest -k <name> -v` (or the full
suite) and read the real output. If a command fails to run at all, that is
BLOCKED, not FAILED.

Do not fix anything. Do not edit any file. If you find a real defect, report
it as FAILED with the raw pytest output as evidence — do not patch it.

## Test cases (PBI-004 — CLI y plataformas)

- TOK-004-C01 - platform claude-code usa Claude 4.8 por defecto
  -> pytest test: test_claude_code_platform_succeeds
- TOK-004-C02 - platform codex usa o200k_base
  -> pytest test: test_codex_platform_succeeds
- TOK-004-C03 - opencode sin --model falla con mensaje claro
  -> pytest test: test_opencode_platform_without_model_fails_cleanly
- TOK-004-C04 - opencode con --model claude-* usa Claude
  -> pytest test: test_opencode_platform_with_claude_model_succeeds
- TOK-004-C05 - antigravity sale con codigo 2 y mensaje planned
  -> pytest test: test_antigravity_platform_fails_with_planned_message

## Output format

Run `uv sync` then `uv run pytest -v` once for the whole suite, and report
the raw output. Then, for each of the 5 cases above, output one line in
exactly this format (no extra prose):

VEREDICTO: <exact case name from the list above> | <PASSED|FAILED|BLOCKED> | <one-line evidence, e.g. "pytest test_x PASSED in 0.01s">

Print the raw pytest output first, then the five VEREDICTO lines, then
nothing else.
